            <fr:title text="An unsharded contracting axis needs no comms">An unsharded contracting axis needs no comms</fr:title>
            <fr:taxon>Card</fr:taxon>
            <fr:meta name="anki">1786546736361</fr:meta>
            <fr:meta name="anki-deck">MLSys</fr:meta>
          </fr:frontmatter>
          <fr:mainmatter>
  <html:p><fr:link href="/XGI0/" title="Sharding" uri="https://kellenkanarios.com/XGI0/" display-uri="XGI0" type="local">Sharded</fr:link> matmul, case 1: neither multiplicand has a sharded contracting dimension. What communication is required, and what is the output sharding?</html:p>
  
  
  
 <html:div class="jms-callout" style="
  margin: 1.1rem 0;
  padding: 0.1rem 0 0.1rem 0.9rem;
  border-left: 2px solid rgb(63, 83, 127);
  "><html:span style="font-weight: 500; color: rgb(63, 83, 127);" /> 
  <html:details><html:summary><html:span class="foldout" style="font-family: var(--sans); font-size: 11pt; font-weight: 500; color: rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.65);">Answer</html:span></html:summary>
  <html:strong>None at all.</html:strong> Each device does a local block matmul and the result is already sharded correctly:

  <fr:tex display="block"><![CDATA[A[I_X, J] \cdot  B[J, K_Y] \to  C[I_X, K_Y]]]></fr:tex>

  The contracting axis <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[J]]></fr:tex> is unsharded, so every device holds complete contracting slices and the computation does not depend on the sharding. The output simply inherits the input shardings.

  The one caveat: this holds only if the two inputs' <html:em>non-contracting</html:em> dimensions are not sharded over the same mesh axis — that case is <fr:link href="/QBBA/" title="A mesh axis may appear only once" uri="https://kellenkanarios.com/QBBA/" display-uri="QBBA" type="local">invalid</fr:link>.
</html:details>
</html:div>



</fr:mainmatter>
